 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Your bill is probably high. But Perth's power prices aren't rising unusually fast, so the bill itself is likely high for another reason.**\n\n**What the ABS data shows (electricity CPI, year-on-year):**\n- **Perth, August 2026:** +2.4%. Perth has been at +2.4–2.5% since April 2026.\n- **Australia (8-capital average), August 2026:** +13.2%. It was +37% in February 2026.\n- **Overall CPI, August 2026:** Perth +4.3%, Australia +4.0%.\n\nSo the cost of living is rising a bit faster in Perth than nationally, but electricity isn't the cause. WA tariffs are government-regulated and move in small annual steps. The east coast has had much bigger jumps.\n\n**Over a longer period:** Perth's electricity price index was 92.86 in June 2023 and 102.44 in August 2026. That's about 10% higher over three years, or roughly 3% a year. Nationally the index went from 93.79 to 113.69 over the same period, about 21% higher.\n\n**Why it might still feel huge:**\n- **Rebates.** Perth's index swung wildly in 2022–2026, for example 14.2 in mid-2022 and 25.7 in early 2025. That looks like one-off government bill credits being applied and then ending, not real price changes. If you got a credit and it has ended, your bill will look much bigger even though the tariff has barely moved.\n- **Seasonal use.** Winter heating and summer air-conditioning can lift your usage a lot.\n- **Cumulative rises.** Every year's increase stacks on the previous one, and overall prices are up about 4% in the past year.\n- **Your own usage or plan.** Check your kWh use against the same period last year. Things like a new appliance, an EV or a pool pump can change a bill a lot.\n\nI can't see your bill, so I can't say which of these applies to you. Comparing your usage in kWh with last year's bill is the quickest check.",
